    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/jobs/operations-manager-physics-observatories-hobart-tas-australia": {
      "title": "Operations Manager",
      "description": "Operations Manager - Physics & Observatories - Hobart, TAS, Australia Skip to main content Visit UTAS My Profile Kiosk mode Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Video Player is loading. Play Video Play Skip Backward Skip Forward Mute Current Time 0:00 / Duration 1:26 Loaded : 0% 0:00 Stream Type LIVE Seek to live, currently behind live LIVE Remaining Time - 1:26 1x Playback Rate Chapters Chapters Descriptions descriptions off , selected Subtitles subtitles settings , opens subtitles settings dialog subtitles off , selected Audio Track Picture-in-Picture Fullscreen This is a modal window. Beginning of dialog window. Escape will cancel and close the window. Text Color White Black Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Opaque Semi-Transparent Text Background Color Black White Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Opaque Semi-Transparent Transparent Caption Area Background Color Black White Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Transparent Semi-Transparent Opaque Font Size 50% 75% 100% 125% 150% 175% 200% 300% 400% Text Edge Style None Raised Depressed Uniform Drop shadow Font Family Proportional Sans-Serif Monospace Sans-Serif Proportional Serif Monospace Serif Casual Script Small Caps Reset Done Close Modal Dialog End of dialog window. Operations Manager - Physics & Observatories 504351 Hobart, TAS Full time Closing on: 18 Oct 2026 Add to favourites View favourites The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Lead a team of technical staff for the safe, reliable and sustainable operation of world-class observatory and research infrastructure. Work across diverse environments, from campus-based facilities to remote observatory sites across Tasmania and interstate. Full time, Two Year Fixed Term role, Based in Southern Tasmania (Cambridge/Sandy Bay) About the opportunity We are seeking an Operations Manager, Physics & Observatories to support the safe, effective and sustainable operation of the University of Tasmania's internationally recognised observatory facilities. These observatories enable world-leading research in astrophysics, geodesy, space science and telecommunications, while contributing to national and international scientific collaborations. Working with a team of technical professionals, researchers and external partners, you will oversee the operation, maintenance and continuous improvement of a diverse network of observatory and research infrastructure across multiple sites. You will contribute to work health and safety, asset and facilities operational and strategic management, risk management, contractor oversight and operational planning, helping to ensure our facilities remain safe, compliant, reliable and fit for purpose. This role offers the opportunity to work across a unique network of observatory facilities and research infrastructure, including travel to sites across Tasmania and interstate that contribute to Australia's astronomy, space science and advanced communications capability. What you’ll do Oversee the day-to-day operation of the University's observatory facilities, ensuring infrastructure, assets and technical systems are safe, reliable and fit for purpose. Support a positive safety culture through the development, implementation and review of safe systems of work, risk assessments and operational procedures. Partner with contractors, service providers and suppliers to ensure maintenance and operational activities are delivered safely, efficiently and in accordance with relevant requirements. Coordinate operational planning, facilities management and continuous improvement initiatives across multiple observatory locations. Collaborate with technical staff, researchers and stakeholders to support research, teaching and industry engagement activities. Contribute to the sustainability, capability and future development of nationally significant observatory facilities and research infrastructure. What we’re looking for: Experience leading teams and managing facilities, infrastructure or technical operations within a research, scientific, educational, industrial, infrastructure or similarly complex operational environment. Demonstrated experience applying work health and safety principles, risk management practices and compliance requirements to support safe and effective operations. Experience working with contractors, service providers and maintenance programs in a regulated environment. Experience in facilities, asset or infrastructure management, including maintenance planning, procurement, lifecycle management or continuous improvement activities. Experience managing budgets, resources and competing priorities. Strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to build effective relationships across a broad range of technical and non-technical stakeholders. A current driver's licence and willingness to travel to observatory and research infrastructure sites, including remote locations. What’s on Offer? As we look toward 2050, we remain committed to fostering excellence, collaboration, and transformative education - preparing graduates to navigate the challenges of a rapidly changing world, and shape its future. While remaining true to our Tasmanian roots, this career defining role will see you significantly contribute to our dedication to making a difference for, and from, Tasmania. In return for your experience, you’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 17% Superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and Car Hire discounts Health Insurance and Banking Novated Leasing Relocation Support Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 8 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $152,033 comprising base salary within the range of $114,282 to $129,943 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role and include an overview of how your previous skills & experience aligns to the requirements of the role For further information about this position, please contact Andrea Shadbolt, Business Manager, School of Natural Sciences Andrea.Shadbolt@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Operations Manager - Physics Observatories.pdf Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 777465b5 Last Name (required) 702643f6 Email (required) cfb5f802 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Learning Adviser (STEM/Numeracy) Hobart, TAS Student Services, Teaching Support Deliver specialist learning development support in STEM-related areas, including numeracy, mathematics, statistics, quantitative reasoning, scientific literacy and data interpretation. Lecturer in English Hobart, TAS. Launceston, TAS Teaching Intensive Shape the future of English education and inspire the next generation of readers, writers and critical thinkers through innovative and engaging learning experiences Teaching Laboratory Technician Hobart, TAS Laboratory & Research Support Play a key role in supporting hands-on teaching and learning, helping to deliver safe, engaging and well-organised laboratory experiences for our students Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 0ab076d1 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Management Management Employment Type Full time Full time Locations Hobart, TAS, Australia Hobart, TAS, Australia First Name (required) 1cf30abc Last Name (required) c2472e89 Email (required) a13d6c4d Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you © 2026  University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Cookie Preferences Copyright Statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
